/* ELEC5620 — quiz bank.
 * Entry shape: { ch, type: "study"|"math", q, opts: [..], answer: <index>, exp }
 */
var ALL_QUIZ_DATA = [

  /* Ch1 */
  { ch: 1, type: "study", q: "Which answer to \"what is a model?\" would earn the mark?", opts: ["A deliberately incomplete representation of a system, built to answer one class of question about it", "A complete and precise description of the system, produced before the code is written", "A simplified version of the system, kept beside the code", "A picture of the system drawn in UML"], answer: 0, exp: "The markable form names both the omission and the purpose it serves. A UML picture names an artefact rather than the relationship, a complete description is by definition not a model, and \"simplified version\" drops the one-class-of-question clause that makes the selectivity judgeable." },
  { ch: 1, type: "study", q: "The lecture nests three boxes: Software Engineering contains Software Design contains Software Architecture. What follows for this unit?", opts: ["Architecture is the code-level view of the same design decisions", "Architecture is design work at the coarsest level of detail — what the parts are and how they talk to each other", "Architecture is a separate discipline from design and replaces it once the code exists", "Architecture is the finest-grained level and therefore includes every design detail"], answer: 1, exp: "Architecture details are a subset of software design, so architecture is the coarsest level of design detail. The other options either separate architecture from design or invert the containment." },
  { ch: 1, type: "study", q: "A team records that the order service calls the payment service, and that no service may call the order service back. Which part of the IEEE 42010 definition is doing the work?", opts: ["fundamental — irrelevant details are omitted", "components — architecture involves decomposition", "relationships and guiding principles — how the parts may be assembled, and the rules later design must obey", "system — a collection of components organized to accomplish a function"], answer: 2, exp: "Naming a permitted call and a forbidden one is a statement about the manner of assembly plus a rule constraining later design and evolution, which is the relationships-and-guiding-principles clause. Decomposition and abstraction are not what the sentence asserts." },
  { ch: 1, type: "study", q: "Over two years, each release adds one small shortcut until ordering, pricing and notification logic are entangled across four services. What has happened?", opts: ["Nothing architectural — the code simply reflects normal staff turnover", "The architecture evolved deliberately, guided by its recorded principles", "Essential complexity increased because the business domain became harder", "Architectural decay: design decisions were gradually corrupted by minor fixes and modifications"], answer: 3, exp: "Decay is the accumulation of individually reasonable changes, caused by the difficulty of comprehending the whole from the details and of enforcing decisions during implementation. It is not one bad decision, and deliberate evolution guided by recorded principles is the healthy case." },
  { ch: 1, type: "study", q: "A developer loses three days to an adapter because the inventory vendor returns availability as a nested array, then has to redo it when the vendor changes the schema. How does the unit classify that?", opts: ["Accidental complexity — the response shape is a property of our chosen solution, and raising the level of abstraction guards against it", "Neither — three days of work is simply normal development cost", "Both — the vendor's schema is essential because the vendor is outside our control", "Essential complexity — deciding which shoe to recommend is inherently difficult"], answer: 0, exp: "Accidental complexity comes from the implementation technology and the methods used, and an integration layer that hides the vendor's REST shape removes it. Fit and ranking trade-offs, by contrast, are essential and no refactoring removes them." },
  { ch: 1, type: "study", q: "You must show that a ShoeModel cannot exist without the Manufacturer that produces it, and that deleting the manufacturer deletes the model. Which notation is correct?", opts: ["Aggregation — a hollow diamond at the manufacturer end", "Composition — a filled diamond at the manufacturer end", "Generalisation — a hollow triangle pointing at the manufacturer", "A plain association with multiplicity 1 at the manufacturer end"], answer: 1, exp: "A part that cannot outlive the whole, where deleting the whole deletes the part, is composition, drawn as the filled diamond at the whole end. A plain association makes no lifetime claim, a hollow diamond is the weak shared ownership of aggregation, and generalisation is a subset-set claim about instances." },
  { ch: 1, type: "study", q: "A modeller must show (a) the order in which messages flow between four services for one scenario and (b) the states a single booking object can be in. Which pair of diagrams fits?", opts: ["(a) state machine, because there is a lifecycle; (b) sequence diagram", "(a) communication diagram, because the wiring is the point; (b) activity diagram", "(a) sequence diagram, because time is the point; (b) state machine, because it is one object changing", "(a) activity diagram, because work flows; (b) sequence diagram"], answer: 2, exp: "Sequence organises interactions by time — lifelines down the page, messages in order — while communication organises the same interaction by structure. For the second question, ask how many objects are in the picture: many flowing is an activity, one changing is a state machine." },
  { ch: 1, type: "math", q: "A tool reports that an association in your diagram connects a class to a use case. Which level of the model-metamodel hierarchy has been violated?", opts: ["None — any two elements may legally be joined by an association", "M0 — the running system does not behave that way", "M1 — the model is describing the wrong domain concept", "M2 — the metamodel defines what an association may connect"], answer: 3, exp: "The metamodel fixes which constructs exist, what properties they have and how they may legally combine, so a construction the metamodel forbids is not a model of the language at all. \"Is this diagram legal?\" is a question about M2, whereas \"is this diagram right?\" is a question about the system in M0." },
  { ch: 1, type: "math", q: "Two transitions leave the same state on the same event: accept() [express] and accept() [international]. What is wrong?", opts: ["The guards must be mutually exclusive, otherwise the machine is ambiguous when both hold", "Both transitions must be merged into a single transition with a combined effect", "The guards must be moved into the effect, because guards may not be boolean", "Nothing — a state may have as many outgoing transitions as it needs"], answer: 0, exp: "Determinism requires at most one enabled transition per state and event: if two guards can be true together the machine has silently become a choice made by whichever branch the implementation tests first. The fix is exclusive guards or an explicit choice pseudostate; guards are boolean and never carry the effect." },
  /* Ch2 */
  { ch: 2, type: "study", q: "Which statement matches the deck's definition of agentic AI?", opts: ["LLMs that generate content such as text and images", "Highly autonomous systems that outperform humans at most economically valuable work, with human-like cognitive abilities", "Any program that calls a large language model through an API", "Software that automates a single specialised task faster than a person"], answer: 1, exp: "Generative AI is the layer underneath — LLMs that generate content — and narrow AI handles specialised tasks, so both are explicitly not agentic in the deck's sense. Calling an LLM API is an implementation detail, not the definition." },
  { ch: 2, type: "study", q: "A team loses a fortnight to a mismatched toolchain version and a build script nobody understands. How does the deck classify that, and what follows?", opts: ["Both — toolchains are essential because the vendor chose them", "Essential complexity — the problem is simply hard", "Accidental complexity — it comes from the technology and the methods, and raising the level of abstraction guards against it", "Neither — project overhead is unavoidable and outside the scope of modelling"], answer: 2, exp: "Brooks' accidental complexity is due to the implementation technology, ineffective planning or a mismatching and immature technology, so it is avoidable in principle, and modelling is the raised level of abstraction that guards against it. Essential complexity is inherent to the problem and stays." },
  { ch: 2, type: "study", q: "Which answer to \"what is an engineering model?\" would earn the mark?", opts: ["Any UML diagram produced by a modelling tool", "A diagram of the code, drawn to document the implementation", "A complete record of everything known about the system", "A selective representation that captures accurately and concisely all of its essential properties of interest for a given set of concerns"], answer: 3, exp: "Every clause is load-bearing: selective (it omits), accurate and concise (it is not a dump), essential (not incidental) and relative to a set of concerns. A complete record contradicts selectivity, a tool's output names an artefact rather than a relationship, and a diagram of the code names neither purpose nor concerns." },
  { ch: 2, type: "study", q: "A team models an online store as a use case diagram and expects to generate the checkout code from it. What does the Week 2 material say?", opts: ["The use case model supports abstraction and communication, not direct code generation", "Purpose is irrelevant once the notation has been chosen correctly", "Code generation is impossible from any UML model, including a state machine", "It is correct: every model supports both abstraction and automation equally"], answer: 0, exp: "Abstraction helps people understand and automation helps tools act, but the tutorial is explicit that a use case diagram mainly supports abstraction and communication. State machines with triggers, guards and effects are the models a tool can act on, and claiming that code generation is impossible from any UML model overstates the limit." },
  { ch: 2, type: "study", q: "A new engineer asks for \"the model\" and is handed a class diagram, a state machine and a deployment diagram. Why is that not a mistake?", opts: ["They are three drafts of the same picture and one of them must be made authoritative", "Each diagram is a view for a different set of concerns, and each is selective by design", "A system may have only one model, so two of the three are redundant", "Views must be disjoint, so the three diagrams cannot overlap"], answer: 1, exp: "A view is the model of the system produced from one viewpoint, and one system has many views that can all be faithful while answering different questions. Views overlap deliberately and must be consistent where they overlap, so disjointness is not the requirement either." },
  { ch: 2, type: "study", q: "What does applying the stereotype «sc_method» to a class actually do?", opts: ["It creates a subclass of the branded class", "It adds a new attribute to the class", "It brands an existing model element with domain meaning drawn from a profile", "It replaces the class name with the tool's internal name"], answer: 2, exp: "A stereotype is a controlled metamodel extension: it adds vocabulary, not structure, which is why the SystemC method/slave/link idioms can be named without changing the model's elements. It adds no attribute and no subclass, and it never alters the name." },
  { ch: 2, type: "math", q: "A team raises the level of abstraction of its development technology, modelling the appointment workflow instead of hand-writing the state handling. According to the week's effort relation, what should happen?", opts: ["Essential effort rises because the model must be reviewed as well", "Total effort is unchanged, because the work simply moves into the model", "Both halves of the effort fall, because the model is simpler than the code", "Accidental effort falls and essential effort is unchanged"], answer: 3, exp: "Raising the level of abstraction reduces accidental complexity and does not touch essential complexity, which is why modelling is a cure for a badly expressed problem and not for a hard one. Review effort is real, but it does not turn essential complexity into accidental." },
  { ch: 2, type: "math", q: "A radio base station codebase is 2,000,000 lines and 87% of it was produced by tools. How many hand-written lines remain, and what does the ratio establish?", opts: ["260,000 hand-written lines, and the ratio measures leverage rather than correctness", "1,740,000 hand-written lines, and the ratio proves the generated code is correct", "260,000 hand-written lines, and the ratio proves the design decisions were all made by the tool", "870,000 hand-written lines, and the ratio shows accidental complexity was eliminated"], answer: 0, exp: "L_gen = 0.87 x 2,000,000 = 1,740,000, so 260,000 lines are hand-written and carry the decisions no transformation could make. The ratio is leverage, not correctness: the deck's own generation slide shows a bug surviving into generated code." },
  { ch: 2, type: "math", q: "A team uses a general drawing tool for its class diagrams and then wonders why it cannot check whether the diagrams are consistent. What is the structural reason?", opts: ["The tool is too simple to draw class diagrams correctly", "A drawing tool stores shapes, so there is no metamodel for a consistency check to reason over", "Drawing tools cannot express multiplicity or stereotypes", "Consistency checking requires code generation, which only a paid tool provides"], answer: 1, exp: "Only a tool that understands the metamodel can validate, transform or generate; a drawing tool gives you the notation and the communication value and stops there. Multiplicity and stereotypes are drawable in a drawing tool, and generation is a separate capability from consistency checking." },
  /* Ch3 */
  { ch: 3, type: "study", q: "Which list is the deck's list of issues with requirement statements?", opts: ["Performance, reliability, accessibility, usability, security", "Clear expectations, reduced risk, faster development, improved maintenance", "Ambiguity, incompleteness, conflict, difference in priority, and perceived stakeholder urgency", "Eliciting, modelling, communicating, agreeing, evolving"], answer: 2, exp: "The five defects are the issues with the statements themselves; the benefits of good requirements, the requirements-engineering activities and the non-functional quality taxonomy are three different lists that do not name a defect." },
  { ch: 3, type: "study", q: "A team writes requirements as one long prose document that mixes desired behaviour, an implementation decision and a response-time target. Which approach is that, and what is the main risk?", opts: ["Feature analysis, and the risk is that the feature model will not support a product family", "Use case analysis, and the risk is that no actor has been identified", "Feature analysis, and the risk is that the model is too operational", "Ad hoc analysis, and the risk is omissions, conflicts, inconsistencies and ambiguities that are hard to detect later"], answer: 3, exp: "Mixing what, how and how well in informal prose is the ad hoc approach, which the deck calls the usual starting point and explicitly unsystematic and error prone. Feature analysis is capability-based and static; use case analysis is operational and abstract by design." },
  { ch: 3, type: "study", q: "In a feature diagram, three sibling sub-features are grouped under a hollow arc. What has the modeller asserted?", opts: ["Exactly one of the three must be selected, and the others must not be", "All three are optional, so any subset may be selected", "All three are mandatory in every product of the line", "At least one of the three must be selected"], answer: 0, exp: "The hollow arc is the alternative (xor) group, used for the e-shop's choice between a standard and a high-standard security policy. The filled arc is the or-group, at least one; mandatory or optional is carried by the filled or hollow circle on the parent end of each edge." },
  { ch: 3, type: "study", q: "A payroll system automatically reports superannuation contributions to the tax office's reporting service. Should that service appear on the use case diagram, and as what?", opts: ["No — external systems belong on a deployment diagram, not a use case diagram", "Yes — as a second actor, because an outside system that interacts with yours is an actor", "Yes — as a use case named Report contributions inside the boundary", "No — the diagram only shows human users of the system"], answer: 1, exp: "An actor is an entity — person, organisation or outside system — that interacts with the system to achieve goals, and the tax office service produces or consumes data across the boundary. Anything inside the boundary is system responsibility, and a use case is a goal, not a participant." },
  { ch: 3, type: "study", q: "When someone in the secure area presses the panic button the system messages the police instead of sounding the alarm; the main success scenario is unaffected. Which relationship models Summon Police?", opts: ["A separate use case with no relationship, since the scenario is unaffected", "include, from Summon Police to Occupy Secure Room, because the base always ends by calling the police", "extend, from Summon Police to Occupy Secure Room, with the condition [panic button pressed] and an extension point", "generalization, with the hollow triangle pointing at Occupy Secure Room"], answer: 2, exp: "If the base is complete and correct without it, and a condition at a defined point weaves it in, the relationship is extend — and extend leaves the extending use case and points at the base. include would assert the base cannot complete without it, and generalization would mean it replaces the base while achieving the same goal." },
  { ch: 3, type: "study", q: "A use case specification says the failure scenario is 4.a {ATM has insufficient cash on hand}, containing 1. Notify user, 2. Log out user. What does the 4.a tell you?", opts: ["It is an optional fourth step that runs only when the ATM has cash", "It is a nested use case named 4.a", "It is a second main success scenario that replaces step 4", "It is the extension scenario for the condition that can interrupt step 4 of the main success scenario"], answer: 3, exp: "Failure scenarios branch off from the point of failure and are indexed by the step they interrupt — 5.a, 7.c — rather than numbered as a separate flow. The label records the step, the letter records the branch, and a.b is not a use case name." },
  { ch: 3, type: "study", q: "Which constraint can a use case diagram not express, and therefore needs another model?", opts: ["The ordering within a scenario, such as \"scan within 30 seconds after the door opens\"", "That the system boundary excludes the payment service", "That an actor participates in a goal", "That a use case is included by another use case"], answer: 0, exp: "The diagram cannot specify anything, cannot show ordering within a scenario, and cannot carry the textual template's goal, precondition, trigger or failure conditions; ordering is exactly what interaction modelling such as a sequence diagram supplies. Participation, include and the boundary are all notation on the diagram." },
  { ch: 3, type: "math", q: "A diagram shows a dashed arrow from Login to Withdraw Cash with no stereotype written on it. What is wrong?", opts: ["Nothing — the arrow simply means that login is used by withdrawing cash", "The relationship is ambiguous: a dashed arrow must carry «include» or «extend», because the stereotype distinguishes them", "It should be a solid line, because generalization uses dashed arrows", "It should carry a multiplicity, because Login appears once per withdrawal"], answer: 1, exp: "The arrowhead and the stereotype together are the claim. include leaves the base and points at the required child, extend leaves the extending use case and points at the base, so an unlabelled dashed arrow between them could be read either way. Generalization is a solid line with a hollow triangle, and use case relationships carry no multiplicity." },
  { ch: 3, type: "math", q: "Which statement is written in a form a test can falsify?", opts: ["The system shall be secure and easy to use", "The system shall respond quickly and behave robustly under load", "When the door is opened while the system is enabled, the system shall sound the audible alarm and send a message to the police if no valid fingerprint is scanned within 30 seconds", "The system shall support fingerprint identification and audible alarms"], answer: 2, exp: "It fills all five slots — subject, trigger, response, object and measure — so a test falls straight out of it. The other three name capabilities or adjectives with no measure, and a requirement is verifiable only when the obligation carries a measure a test could falsify." },
  { ch: 3, type: "math", q: "The secure-access specification says a valid fingerprint must be scanned within 30 seconds of the door opening. How should this be classified?", opts: ["Neither — it is a design constraint that belongs in the architecture, not in requirements", "Functional, because it names a number and therefore an obligation", "Functional, because it describes what the system does when the door opens", "Non-functional: it constrains how the identification function must perform rather than adding a new behaviour"], answer: 3, exp: "Functional requirements describe what the system does and are realised by use cases; non-functional ones constrain how it does so — latency, reliability, accessibility and the rest. Note the deck's caution that for very many systems these are not separable concerns: move the latency bound far enough and the functional requirement itself changes." },
  /* Ch4 */
  { ch: 4, type: "study", q: "A team fixes three defects by adding direct calls between two modules that the architecture says must not know about each other. Two years later nobody can say where the boundary runs. What has happened?", opts: ["Architectural decay: the architecture was corrupted by a series of locally reasonable fixes", "Nothing, because the code still compiles and the tests still pass", "The model of computation changed from layered to point-to-point", "The architecture evolved, and evolution means the design changes as requirements arrive"], answer: 0, exp: "Decay is change that is locally reasonable and globally destructive, caused by difficulty perceiving the architecture and difficulty enforcing its decisions; deliberate evolution guided by recorded principles is the healthy case. Compiling code is not evidence that the architecture survived." },
  { ch: 4, type: "study", q: "Which answer to \"what is an architecture?\" would earn the mark?", opts: ["The elements of a system and the relationships between them", "The fundamental concepts or properties of a system in its environment embodied in its elements, relationships and the principles of its design and evolution", "The document that describes the system's modules and their interfaces", "The set of UML diagrams produced during the design phase"], answer: 1, exp: "The definition has three parts, and the principles are as load-bearing as the elements and relationships, which is why listing only the elements and relationships is incomplete. The document is the architecture description — the work product that expresses the architecture — and a diagram set is a description, not the architecture itself." },
  { ch: 4, type: "study", q: "Which answer to \"what is a viewpoint?\" would earn the mark?", opts: ["A diagram drawn for one stakeholder group", "A representation of the whole system from the perspective of a related set of concerns", "The set of concerns and the representation techniques used to describe the architecture to address those concerns", "The hardware node on which each component is deployed"], answer: 2, exp: "IEEE 1471 separates the two words: the viewpoint identifies the concerns and the representation techniques, and the view is the result of applying a viewpoint to a particular system. The distractor that defines a view — a representation of the whole system from the perspective of a related set of concerns — is the other half of the same pair, and a diagram is one possible rendering of a view." },
  { ch: 4, type: "study", q: "A team documents the threads, message ordering and synchronisation between its agents, but nothing about which class holds the patient record or which server runs the model. Which views are missing?", opts: ["Only the +1 scenario view, since it ties the other four together", "The development view, because packages are what organise the agents", "The process view, because concurrency is an architectural concern", "The logical view and the physical (deployment) view"], answer: 3, exp: "Responsibilities and conceptual components are the logical view, and execution placement is the physical view, so both are absent. Concurrency was precisely what the team did document, and the scenario view is a vertical slice, not a substitute for a missing view." },
  { ch: 4, type: "study", q: "During analysis a team writes: decompose the consultation domain into scheduling, clinical knowledge and notification, because each has different data, latency and change rates. Which key analysis issue is this?", opts: ["Domain identification and partitioning", "Selecting suitable methods and tools for validation", "Requirements identification and specification", "Stakeholder viewpoint identification"], answer: 0, exp: "Partitioning the problem into areas of conceptual and technological homogeneity is exactly the third core analysis issue, and the differing data, latency and change rates are what make it defensible. Requirements specification asks what must be built, and viewpoint identification asks whose concerns they are." },
  { ch: 4, type: "study", q: "A pipeline of perception, then reasoning, then action is redesigned so each stage fires as soon as its input is available, letting several stages run at once. Which change has been made?", opts: ["The system moved to a procedural (control-flow) model of computation", "The system moved to a data-flow model of computation", "The decomposition changed from three components to four", "The system moved to a structure-dominant model of computation"], answer: 1, exp: "In data flow, components are functional transforms triggered by the availability of input data, so the execution order is determined by data availability and fine-grained parallelism becomes possible. Procedural models trigger on the completion of a predecessor and fix the call graph in advance; structure-dominant models trigger on stimuli." },
  { ch: 4, type: "study", q: "A team puts the retry-and-restart rules for five services inside each service, and exposes them through each service's public API. Which design principle has been violated, and what did it cost?", opts: ["Nothing — control is supposed to live with the function it protects", "Centralising control, because the rules should have been split across the services", "Separating control from function: policies were placed in functional components and exposed through functional interfaces", "The model of computation, because retries imply asynchronous execution"], answer: 2, exp: "The mechanical test the deck gives is whether a change to a control policy forces an edit to a functional component's public interface; here it does, so control and function were not separated, and the components cannot be reused because they carry someone else's rules. Policies belong in control components and mechanisms inside the controlled ones." },
  { ch: 4, type: "study", q: "A team adopts layering and immediately hits the question \"which layer does the audit trail belong to?\". Which statement is correct?", opts: ["The audit trail should become a layer, because cross-cutting concerns always become layers", "The question cannot arise, because layers are defined by the requirements document", "Layering is the wrong pattern, because a pattern must answer every design question it raises", "That question is the known disadvantage of layering, and the pattern still delivers reduced complexity, modularity, reusability and maintainability"], answer: 3, exp: "Every pattern is a context, a problem and a reused solution with a published disadvantage, and \"how to define the layers?\" is layering's. A pattern is named together with the context that makes it valid and the price it charges, so a question the pattern raises is not a refutation of the pattern." },
  { ch: 4, type: "math", q: "A team proves that their implementation conforms to its specification and, separately, that the specification satisfies every recorded stakeholder requirement. What have they established?", opts: ["Verification and validation: conformance of the artefact to the specification, and adequacy of the specification to the needs", "Neither, because only formal methods can establish either", "Validation only, because the specification is what the user asked for", "Verification only, because the second proof is also about the specification"], answer: 0, exp: "Verification is I satisfies Sp — building the product right — and validation is Sp satisfies N — building the right product. The two are independent, which is exactly why both proofs are needed and why a passing test suite validates nothing about the requirements." },
  { ch: 4, type: "math", q: "Six clinical subsystems must be connected. How many links does a fully connected point-to-point architecture need, and what does the mediated alternative cost?", opts: ["12 links against 6, because each subsystem needs an in link and an out link", "15 links against 6 for a mediator, which then becomes a single point of failure and a throughput ceiling", "36 links against 6, because every pair needs two directions", "6 links against 15, because the mediator multiplies the connections"], answer: 1, exp: "E_p2p = n(n-1)/2 = 6x5/2 = 15 against E_mediator = n = 6. The saving is bought with a new critical component, which is precisely the statement an architectural principle such as \"no single point of failure in critical components\" has to rule on." },
  /* Ch5 */
  { ch: 5, type: "study", q: "One object prince : Adult holds \"Prince\" at t=1958 and \"The Artist Formerly Known As Prince\" at t=1993. What does the model's concept of identity say?", opts: ["Identity changes when an object is reclassified, so the type change would have created a new object", "The attribute slot changed, so this must be two different objects", "Identity is acquired at creation and is what the attribute slots are attached to, so it is the same object", "Identity is the value of the object's key attribute, so the identity changed with the name"], answer: 2, exp: "Object identity is an immanent property acquired upon creation and retained unchanged throughout the object's lifetime; it does not change when attribute values change and it does not change under type reclassification. A key attribute that happens to be unique is still only an attribute slot." },
  { ch: 5, type: "study", q: "Why is Association a classifier?", opts: ["Because every element in a class diagram is a classifier by definition", "Because it has a name and exactly two ends", "Because it is drawn as a line between two classes", "Because its instances are links, those links share the association's ends and owned properties, and the collection of links can be divided into sub-collections by association specialisation"], answer: 3, exp: "The definition of a classifier has two clauses — shared characteristics and divisibility into sub-collections — and an association satisfies both, which is why it can carry its own attributes as an association class. Naming its shape or its ends quotes only the first clause." },
  { ch: 5, type: "study", q: "In the Adult 1..2 parents — 0..* children Child model, what exactly does the 1..2 at the parents end constrain?", opts: ["How many Adult instances a single Child may be linked to", "How many Adult objects may exist in the whole system", "How many parents a Child may gain during its lifetime", "How many children a single Adult may have"], answer: 0, exp: "A multiplicity is read from the class at the other end: the 1..2 written at the parents end bounds the number of Adult instances reachable from one Child. Reading it left to right — as a bound on an Adult's children — is the classic reading error, and the 0..* at the children end is the bound that applies to an Adult." },
  { ch: 5, type: "study", q: "An association end carries 0..* with no modifier strings. What has the modeller asserted about that collection?", opts: ["That the collection may be empty and is ordered, since * implies a sequence", "That it is an unordered set: elements are unique and the order is not meaningful", "That it is an ordered sequence of unique elements", "That duplicates are permitted, because nothing was said about uniqueness"], answer: 1, exp: "{unique} defaults to true and {ordered} defaults to false, so saying nothing asserts a set and a design that needs duplicates must write {nonunique} explicitly. The deck's {unique, ordered} at the parents end is a real extra claim precisely because ordered is not the default." },
  { ch: 5, type: "study", q: "A diagram puts a filled diamond at the String end of Person 0..1 name — 1 String. What has the modeller committed to?", opts: ["That a String is a kind of Person and inherits its features", "That the name can be shared between persons, but weakly", "That the Person owns the String and removing the Person removes it", "That the String attribute is instantiated at the same moment as the Person"], answer: 2, exp: "Composition implies ownership with deletion semantics: when the owner is removed, all its owned elements are removed with it. Nothing in the notation says when a part is created, and weak shared ownership would be the hollow diamond — which in standard UML denotes the same links as a plain association." },
  { ch: 5, type: "study", q: "The tutorial asks you to extend the Adult/Child model to show an Adult's sons and daughters explicitly. What is the honest answer?", opts: ["Replace the children association with two 0..* associations and drop the gender attribute", "Add an association class between Adult and Child that records the gender", "Add two associations, one for sons and one for daughters", "Add no new association: Child already carries gender : [M, F], so sons and daughters are subsets of Adult.children selected by that attribute"], answer: 3, exp: "Two extra associations would state the same links twice, break the meaning of the 0..* multiplicity and lose the fact that the two subsets are disjoint and together cover the whole collection. If they must be navigable, the correct move is derived ends marked {subsets children}." },
  { ch: 5, type: "study", q: "A model has a general BankAccount accounts 1..* — 1..* customers BankCustomer association plus a specialised CorporateAccount C-accounts 1..* — 1 CorporateCustomer whose end is marked {subsets accounts}. Which configuration can the model not represent?", opts: ["A CorporateAccount linked to a PrivateCustomer, because the specialised association's class pair excludes it", "A PrivateCustomer linked to three PersonalAccounts", "A PersonalAccount linked to a PrivateCustomer", "A CorporateAccount linked to a CorporateCustomer"], answer: 0, exp: "A specialised association is restricted to a tighter class pair and a tighter multiplicity, and its links are a subset of the general association's, so a corporate account linked to a private customer is unrepresentable rather than merely unusual. The other three are ordinary links the general 1..* — 1..* association permits." },
  { ch: 5, type: "study", q: "A modeller puts WebServer.read(id) and Library.read(id) on a single port of one component. According to the deck, why is this a problem?", opts: ["It prevents the component from providing any interface at all", "The two interfaces declare a conflicting operation, and the state-machine semantics become unclear, so separate ports per interface are much less problematic", "It is illegal in UML, because a port may provide exactly one interface", "It couples the component directly to the implementations of both interfaces"], answer: 1, exp: "The deck gives three reasons against multiple interfaces per port: potential conflicts and the need for a disambiguation mechanism, unclear semantics when the interfaces carry protocol state machines, and the fact that separate ports are simply easier. A port may provide and require more than one interface, and using a port is precisely how a component avoids coupling to its collaborators." },
  { ch: 5, type: "math", q: "A model shows Pilot connected to a lollipop labelled JetEngineControl. What is actually being asserted?", opts: ["That JetEngineControl is a superclass of Pilot", "That a single instance of the interface is attached to the Pilot", "That a classifier realises the interface and the Pilot uses its services", "That Pilot depends on JetEngineControl in the loose dependency sense"], answer: 2, exp: "The lollipop is not an interface instance: it is a combination of an interface and a realisation relationship, one icon standing for two model elements, which is why an examiner can ask you to expand it. Interfaces declare features only and cannot have instances, which rules out reading the lollipop as an interface instance." },
  { ch: 5, type: "math", q: "A modeller writes context Order inv minOneLine: self.lines->size() >= 1 on top of an association whose lines end already carries 1..*. What has happened?", opts: ["The invariant overrides the multiplicity and silently makes the end optional", "The constraint is illegal, because size() may only be used in global expressions", "The invariant converts the association into a composition by binding the lifetime", "The invariant restates the lower bound the multiplicity already carries, so nothing new is constrained"], answer: 3, exp: "A cardinality bound is an invariant, so children : [1..2] and self.children->size() >= 1 and self.children->size() <= 2 are two spellings of one law. The value of an invariant is that it can say what no multiplicity symbol can draw — a sum, a coupling between two associations, or an acyclicity rule." },
  /* Ch6 */
  { ch: 6, type: "study", q: "A team must show which objects exchange which messages, and in what order, for one booking scenario. Which formalism is written for exactly that?", opts: ["An interaction, drawn as a sequence diagram", "A catalogue of the actions available in the system", "An activity diagram with one swimlane per participant", "A state machine for the booking object"], answer: 0, exp: "Interactions focus on the communications between collaborating instances via messages, and the sequence form puts vertical position for time and lifelines for the participants. A state machine is one object's event-driven life, and an activity shows order and responsibility rather than message exchange." },
  { ch: 6, type: "study", q: "Which answer to \"what is an action?\" would earn the mark?", opts: ["A behaviour composed of a coordinated flow of steps", "A single atomic step within an activity, executed by an object, that is not further decomposed inside that activity", "The behaviour executed when a state machine takes a transition", "A condition held over time while the object waits"], answer: 1, exp: "An action is atomic within its activity, is executed by some object, may change variables or slots, and its successors are determined by control flow or data flow. A coordinated flow of steps is an activity, the behaviour executed on taking a transition is that transition's effect, and a condition held over time describes a state." },
  { ch: 6, type: "study", q: "An active object is executing a long action when three messages arrive. Under run-to-completion semantics, what happens?", opts: ["The highest-priority message interrupts the action and the rest are queued", "The messages interrupt the action immediately and are handled concurrently with it", "The messages are queued and only considered at the next receive action", "The messages are discarded because the object is busy"], answer: 2, exp: "RTC means any messages arriving between successive receive actions are queued and only considered on the next receive, so the current step always finishes first and no observer can see a half-updated state. Priority changes which queued event is selected, not whether an event may be handled inside another." },
  { ch: 6, type: "study", q: "A transition carries bid [value < 100] /reject. Which statement about the guard is correct?", opts: ["The guard is the trigger, so the transition fires whenever the guard changes", "The guard is optional and may be omitted, because the trigger already determines eligibility", "The guard may update value so that later guards see the new figure", "The guard must be side-effect free: it may read value, not change it"], answer: 3, exp: "Guards are Boolean predicates for the conditional execution of transitions and must be side-effect free — anything that must be computed belongs in the transition's effect, which is why the deck's choice example computes gain in the effect. The trigger is the event; a guard is an additional condition." },
  { ch: 6, type: "study", q: "Selling receives bid, computes gain := calculatePotentialGain(value), and then branches on [gain >= 200], [gain < 100] and the middle case. Which pseudostate is that, and why?", opts: ["A choice pseudostate, because the guards are evaluated when the choice point is reached, after the incoming transition's effect has run", "A completion transition, because the effect ran first", "A group transition, because the branches are drawn on the composite state", "A junction, because three guards leave one point"], answer: 0, exp: "A junction decides with the values the system had before the transition, so it can only render a decision tree over existing values; a choice decides with the values the transition just produced. The two draw as the same diamond, so only the position of the effect tells you which semantics apply." },
  { ch: 6, type: "study", q: "A modeller models a web server and its three worker processes as four orthogonal regions of one state machine. According to the deck, what is wrong with that?", opts: ["It is wrong because a state machine may not have more than two regions", "Region concurrency models different viewpoints of one entity, not concurrent objects, which need active objects and message queues", "Nothing: orthogonal regions are the standard notation for concurrent objects", "It is wrong because each region must respond to a different event"], answer: 1, exp: "Orthogonality captures different concurrent views of the same entity, so the object is in exactly one state of each region at once and every region sees the same event; the deck names the misuse of regions for concurrent objects as the trap. If two objects run at the same time and exchange messages, the notation is active objects with message queues." },
  { ch: 6, type: "study", q: "A composite state has an off/ group transition and its substate has an off/ transition too. The machine is in the substate and off arrives. What happens?", opts: ["The group transition fires because it is written on the outer state", "The machine is ambiguous and the choice is left to the implementation", "The substate's transition fires; the group transition is not taken", "Both transitions fire, the innermost first and then the group one"], answer: 2, exp: "Two or more transitions may share a trigger, and the innermost one wins — the substate is deeper than the composite state, so the group transition quietly stops applying there without being edited. That is exactly what makes group transitions a convenience rather than a hazard." },
  { ch: 6, type: "math", q: "A state machine shows a transition whose label reads 1sec / with nothing after the slash. What is it?", opts: ["A completion transition with an implicit trigger", "A guard with no trigger, taken whenever the condition holds", "A self-transition whose effect is inherited from the state's entry action", "A time-event trigger with no effect"], answer: 3, exp: "The label has three independent parts: the trigger is the time event 1sec, the guard is absent, and nothing after the slash means there is no effect. A completion transition is the opposite case — the trigger is implicit, generated when a nested state machine terminates, which is why nothing is written before the slash." },
  { ch: 6, type: "math", q: "A transition leaves S1::S11 and targets S2::S21, where S11 is nested in S1 and S21 is nested in S2. Which action is executed immediately after exS11?", opts: ["exS1, the exit action of the outer source state", "The transition's effect, because exits and entries bracket it", "enS2, because the target side is entered next", "enS21, because the target is the innermost nested state"], answer: 0, exp: "Exit actions run from the innermost active state outwards, stopping below the least common ancestor, then the effect runs, then entries run inwards from the least common ancestor to the target. Here the LCA is above both S1 and S2, so the sequence is exS11, exS1, act1, enS2, enS21." },
  { ch: 6, type: "math", q: "A scenario must show that a cancel message may arrive between two booking messages, and that the answer depends on which object has already recorded the booking. Which model is the right primary choice?", opts: ["An activity diagram with a swimlane per participant", "A sequence diagram, supported by a state machine for the object whose state decides the answer", "An interaction overview diagram on its own", "A state machine for the whole system, since the workflow spans several objects"], answer: 1, exp: "The interaction shows which reply can be lost and where the cancel message lands between the booking messages; the state machine gives the object a state in which a late reply is recognisable as stale. An activity diagram shows where cancellation may interrupt the workflow but cannot express the message dependence, and a whole-system workflow is not one object's lifetime." },
  /* Ch7 */
  { ch: 7, type: "study", q: "A class declares both an attribute balance and an operation balance(). The model is validated. What does UML say?", opts: ["It is invalid unless the operation is renamed, because overloading applies only to operations", "The model is invalid: names inside a namespace must be unique", "It is legal, because names in a namespace are not necessarily unique — uniqueness is preferred, and a qualified name disambiguates", "It is legal only because one of the two elements has an empty name"], answer: 2, exp: "UML tolerates two operations with the same name but different parameter sets and an attribute colliding with an operation, so a bare name is not an identifier and a tool must disambiguate by signature or qualified name. Claiming that names must be unique is the answer the deck explicitly warns against." },
  { ch: 7, type: "study", q: "A class Account declares # auditTrail and - salt. A subclass SavingsAccount in the same package tries to reference both. What happens?", opts: ["It can reference neither, because both members are non-public", "It can reference both, because both classes are in the same package", "It can reference salt but not auditTrail", "It can reference auditTrail but not salt"], answer: 3, exp: "Protected's audience is the extended namespace — the owning namespace plus everything that extends it — so a subclass may reach it wherever it lives. Private's audience is only the owning namespace, and being in the same package does not widen it; that would be package visibility, written ~." },
  { ch: 7, type: "study", q: "Why does the deck insist that a package is not intended to model anything?", opts: ["Because it is an organisational device analogous to a file-system folder — a namespace, not a claim about the system", "Because only a Model may carry a viewpoint attribute", "Because a package represents a run-time container of instances", "Because packages cannot own classes or other model elements"], answer: 0, exp: "A package groups the parts of a model so that a human can find them, and the moment it is presented as a domain concept the model has smuggled an implementation decision into the analysis. A package does own classes, interfaces, data types and other packages, and the run-time container is what composition expresses." },
  { ch: 7, type: "study", q: "What is the only substantive difference between a Model and an ordinary package?", opts: ["It carries the tag modelLibrary", "It has one additional attribute, the viewpoint, and is drawn with a triangle icon", "It must be the outermost package and can never be contained in another", "It may contain private elements, which a package may not"], answer: 1, exp: "A Model is a package plus the viewpoint attribute, and that attribute is a real one because it distinguishes two models of the same system. The modelLibrary is the opposite case — a tag with no special semantics — and a Model is typically top level but can still be contained in another package." },
  { ch: 7, type: "study", q: "Namespace X owns A and C and imports namespace Y, which owns +B, +C, -D and +E. Which statement about the result is correct?", opts: ["X contains A, C and E only, because the colliding C is overwritten", "X now owns every element it can name from Y", "X can name Y's public elements, including the qualified name Y::C, but does not own them and cannot see D", "X can name D as well, because import copies the whole target namespace"], answer: 2, exp: "Names(X) = Own(X) union Pub(Y), which gives A, B, C, Y::C and E; the colliding C is written in its qualified form so that both remain addressable. Private members never enter the union, and import makes names available without transferring ownership." },
  { ch: 7, type: "study", q: "The reporting package needs the domain types for its own internal use but must not re-export them to its clients. Which relationship should it use?", opts: ["merge, because it combines the contents into one definition", "An element import with a local visibility of +, so the alias is public", "import, because it brings the public contents across", "access, because the names become available to the accessing namespace without being re-exported"], answer: 3, exp: "Access is the private-import variant: a third namespace sees the accessing namespace's own public names and does not inherit its access to the target. Import would put the names on the importing namespace's public surface, merge is for combining two models of the same thing, and a local visibility of + does exactly what the team must avoid." },
  { ch: 7, type: "study", q: "In the Smart Health Agent case study, Health Data/Profile merges Generic User Data. Why is merge rather than import right here?", opts: ["Because the two packages describe overlapping user data and the model must end up with one combined definition", "Because import cannot cross a model-library boundary", "Because merge is the only relationship that gives access to private elements", "Because merge is the weaker form and keeps the two packages independent"], answer: 0, exp: "Import makes the target's public names available and leaves the definitions separate, whereas merge combines contents so that the model keeps a single definition of each element. No relationship reaches private elements, and merge is not a weaker form of import." },
  { ch: 7, type: "math", q: "A class Report sits in package Analytics and inherits from Document in package Core. Document has a # footer member and a ~ layout member. Can Report reference them?", opts: ["Both, because inheritance makes all members of the superclass reachable", "Yes for footer, no for layout", "Yes for layout, no for footer", "Neither, because both members are non-public"], answer: 1, exp: "Answer by quoting the audience and testing membership: protected's audience is the extended namespace — itself plus everything that extends it — so a subclass qualifies wherever it lives, while package visibility's audience is the enclosing package and Report is outside it. The four kinds are not a total order, which is why the two answers differ." },
  { ch: 7, type: "math", q: "The Week 7 deck lists the manifestations of the namespace concept in UML. Which element is not on that list?", opts: ["Behaviour", "Package", "Association", "State"], answer: 2, exp: "The deck's list is Package, Class, State and Behaviour: a class's features form a namespace and a composite state owns its substates, its entry and exit behaviours and its internal transitions. An association is a classifier whose instances are links, not a namespace manifestation named in the deck." },
  /* Ch8 */
  { ch: 8, type: "study", q: "The Week 8 lecture lists agile practices and then asks whether you will use every one of them. What is required instead?", opts: ["Nothing: if you run iterations you are agile by definition", "Only the practices the Product Owner selects at Sprint Planning", "Every practice on the list, because Scrum mandates them", "Agile values — the practices are a menu, not a checklist, and development in iterations without the rest is not agile"], answer: 3, exp: "The deck's version is \"don't do Agile, be Agile\":  the values are what govern, and running iterations without them is the failure mode the lecture names. The Product Owner does select work, but it is the values, not a subset of practices, that decide whether the project is agile." },
  { ch: 8, type: "study", q: "A Scrum team finishes Sprint Planning and management then asks for one extra screen to be added during the Sprint. What should happen?", opts: ["It goes on the Product Backlog, not into the running Sprint", "The Product Owner re-estimates the whole backlog first", "It is added if the burndown chart shows spare capacity", "It is added to the Sprint, since priorities can change at any time"], answer: 0, exp: "Management does not add new work to a running Sprint; new items join the Product Backlog, which is open to new items at any time, and the team's estimates are the team's to make and are not changed by managers or customers. Priorities can change, but they change the backlog, not the timebox." },
  { ch: 8, type: "study", q: "Which artefact would agile modelling keep, according to the Week 8 material?", opts: ["A design document written for completeness and filed for the unit's records", "A class model the code generator consumes and the team updates with each change", "A diagram that restates the current source code in boxes", "A deployment diagram for an environment nobody will provision"], answer: 1, exp: "Keep a model only when it changes a decision, has a named reader and has an update or delete point — here the generator is a reader and the update happens with the code. Completeness is not a purpose, restating the code answers no live question, and an unprovisioned environment fails both purpose and lifetime." },
  { ch: 8, type: "study", q: "The increment is \"registered users can manage their notification preferences\". Which question makes a class model earn its place?", opts: ["Whether the model matches the deployment diagram drawn in Week 4", "Whether every class in the system has been drawn with all its attributes", "Whether a preference belongs to the user or to a separate Subscription, because that decision changes the database and the API", "Whether the class diagram can be generated into code this Sprint"], answer: 2, exp: "The purpose test asks which decision the model changes: here it is ownership of the preference, so one class box each plus an association with multiplicity 1 to 0..* and a uniqueness invariant is the right size. Redrawing the whole system and matching unrelated views are ceremony for this Sprint." },
  { ch: 8, type: "study", q: "A team draws a sequence diagram for a flow that has not changed in three Sprints, and no one opens it. Which of the three keep-or-drop tests does it fail?", opts: ["Only the purpose test, because the diagram is still accurate", "None: an accurate diagram is always worth keeping", "Only the lifetime test", "Purpose and audience — there is no live decision and no reader"], answer: 3, exp: "Keep = purpose and audience and lifetime, a conjunction, so a model with no decision to change and no reader is dropped even if it is accurate and even if someone promises to maintain it. The stale-artefact failure the week warns about is what happens when audience and lifetime are assumed rather than named." },
  { ch: 8, type: "study", q: "Where does a discovery made while coding belong in an agile model-driven process?", opts: ["Back into the model, in the same change as the code fix", "Into the retrospective notes, for the next release", "Into a separate document, so the model stays as designed", "In the code only — the model was agreed at planning time"], answer: 0, exp: "The reverse step of the round trip is deliberate: the learning is folded back into the model in the same change rather than left in the code as a private truth. The loop is closed when the reverse step reproduces the model exactly, and leaving the difference in the code is what turns the loop into a race." },
  { ch: 8, type: "study", q: "The Week 8 tutorial draws Track Order and Order Processing with «access» dependencies in both directions. What does the Week 8 material say about it?", opts: ["It is required whenever two packages exchange data", "A two-way dependency means the packages cannot be understood, tested or released independently — not automatically wrong, but the cost must be named", "It is a modelling error and one arrow must be deleted immediately", "It is free, because access does not re-export the imported names"], answer: 1, exp: "The tutorial draws the dual dependency deliberately, and the markable observation is the cost: a change to the tracking display can force a change in order processing and the reverse. The smallest repair is an interface the tracker publishes and the other package consumes, after which the dependency points one way." },
  { ch: 8, type: "math", q: "Which statement about estimation matches the Week 8 material?", opts: ["The Product Owner estimates size and the team sets priority", "Managers estimate each backlog item so that the plan is consistent across teams", "The team estimates each item, and its estimates are not changed by managers or customers", "Estimates are set at the Daily Scrum and revised every day"], answer: 2, exp: "Managers and customers set priority together with the Product Owner, while the team estimates size and effort — and even if the others dislike the estimates they are not allowed to change them. The Daily Scrum is fifteen minutes for obstacles, with no problem solving in the meeting." },
  { ch: 8, type: "math", q: "A generator produces a class from the model, a developer adds a validation method directly to that file, and the next generation removes it. Which round-trip condition was violated?", opts: ["The test suite was not derived from the model", "The loop was not checked inside the iteration", "The transformation was not deterministic", "The model was not the single source for everything the generator owns"], answer: 3, exp: "A usable round trip needs a deterministic transformation, the model as the single source for everything the generator owns, and every manual edit either marked and protected or moved back into the model. Hand-editing generated output breaks the second condition, which is why the regeneration silently loses the fix." },
];
